he commit lands and that upload is ended together with the part it has just committed. Closing that needs a change to the upload protocol (proposal 0016) that this release does not make; it is a known gap, and the follow-up that closes it must land before uploads are created in production. Each commit is measured against the batch budgets before it is sent and never split. An operator can run exactly one such pass and exit with `wyrd custodian --reap-abandoned-uploads`: it needs no `--endpoints`, reads and writes metadata only, and drains what it ended record by record, holding for a whole-fleet pass anything that could only be retired by listing the fleet; it exits non-zero when anything was held, a commit's outcome is unknown or the profile check refused, so an open upload under another clock keeps it failing until an operator ends that upload. It is refused before it opens the store when given a flag that selects work it does not run — `--endpoints`, `--ids`, `--failure-domains` or `--gc-expired-pending` — rather than ignoring the flag, and when its `--data-dir` holds no local metadata store — no file there, or an empty one — which it never creates: a store made on the spot would be reported as holding nothing to reap. Its last line names the store it ran on: the local file, or the TiKV PD endpoints or the FoundationDB cluster file it connected with.
+   A multipart upload whose client went silent — never aborted, never completed — is ended by the **multipart reaper** (proposal 0016 decision 6), which runs in the same repair-loop pass, after reconstruction and before the terminal deletes and the obligation walk, so an upload it ends is drained in the same pass. A reconstruction failure does not skip them: the pass still reports it, but only after the reaper and the drain have had their turn, so a fault reading the repair queue cannot keep abandoned uploads holding their admission slots. It first compares the budget profile the admission record stores with its own and, if they differ or the record will not decode, stops before reading any upload and names the mismatch for an operator — and the terminal deletes and the obligation walk do not run in that pass either, since they build the staged references that profile sizes, so no upload's records change in that pass. It reads the admission record once more as the last thing it does, just before those legs run, so a record installed or replaced under another profile while it worked — a first upload created by a differently sized gateway — stops them the same way. With no admission record at all nothing was ever admitted, so it judges no upload. If the record is still missing at its last read while an upload is listed — or the listing cannot be read — the record is lost and the profile those uploads were admitted under is unknown, so the pass is refused as for a mismatch, and the terminal deletes and the obligation walk do not run either. A record that was there at the first read and is gone at the last is lost in the same way — nothing deletes it — and refuses the pass the same way, whatever the pass did before that read. A refusal at the first read says that the reaper refused before acting; one at the last read of a pass that had judged says how many uploads it had already ended — they stay ended, under the profile the first read matched — and the pass's counts a