he commit lands and that upload is ended together with the part it has just committed. Closing that needs a change to the upload protocol (proposal 0016) that this release does not make; it is a known gap, and the follow-up that closes it must land before uploads are created in production. Each commit is measured against the batch budgets before it is sent and never split. An operator can run exactly one such pass and exit with `wyrd custodian --reap-abandoned-uploads`: it needs no `--endpoints`, reads and writes metadata only, and drains what it ended record by record, holding for a whole-fleet pass anything that could only be retired by listing the fleet; it exits non-zero when anything was held, a commit's outcome is unknown or the profile check refused, so an open upload under another clock keeps it failing until an operator ends that upload. It is refused before it opens the store when given a flag that selects work it does not run — `--endpoints`, `--ids`, `--failure-domains` or `--gc-expired-pending` — rather than ignoring the flag, and when its `--data-dir` holds no local metadata store — no file there, or an empty one — which it never creates: a store made on the spot would be reported as holding nothing to reap. Its last line names the store it ran on: the local file, or the TiKV PD endpoints or the FoundationDB cluster file it connected with. The reaper runs on every startup shape of the ordinary role, not only on a healthy fleet. With a fleet it rides each interval's repair-loop pass over whatever part of the fleet is reachable, so a server down at startup or unreachable this pass defers garbage collection but not the reaper; the drain after it still waits for a pass that sees the whole fleet before it retires anything by listing the fleet. With no `--endpoints` the role has nothing to reconstruct but does not exit: it runs the reaper alone every interval, record by record, until it is stopped. Each of those passes is bounded in time; on a networked store a pass past the bound is abandoned and a new one runs next interval, which resumes from what the store holds. When `--endpoints` are given and none of them is reachable at startup, the role runs exactly one reaper pass under the leadership it already holds and then fails as before, with a non-zero exit and the same diagnostic, so a supervisor restarts it; it is not kept up on the empty fleet, because the role never dials a server again after startup. The restore one-shot, `--reconcile-after-restore`, runs no such pass: on a just-restored store the restore pass is the first to act, and a restore run that fails on an empty fleet leaves the store as it found it.
 3. **Propagate across zones (L3).** If the object had replicas, the deletion propagates through the replica catalog: each holding zone drops its catalog record and its GC reclaims the local fragments. Until propagation completes, a *stale-tolerant* read in a lagging zone may still see the old object, bounded by replication lag (section 8.1); consistency-sensitive reads route to the home zone and never do.
 4. **Prove it (audit + crypto-erase).** The deletion is recorded in the append-only audit/event log — the operator's compliance and GDPR-deletion story (section 8.3). Where per-tenant envelope encryption is enabled, dropping the object/tenant key crypto-shreds the data immediately, making it unrecoverable independent of when GC runs (section 8.5).
